From 82307df9eb56a3d342a6d833a553424e729e1555 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 18:33:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0319 -- legacy pathfinder: skip the mask
 compare on an unchanged temp mask; flood by labels

WHY: with 0317, a walk request on the S16 fixture still spends most of
its 486 time outside the Dijkstra loop (dosags docs/lab/s8.md, PROF16:
the lc 562 click tick is 201 ms sound off). The biggest parts are
`__find_route` 42% (the 0211 cache's 64 KB compare) and `big_move` 17%
(the 64 KB copy of the normalised mask, made on every request only so
Allegro's floodfill can mark the source's region), plus the flood
itself.

WHAT, exact by construction (DOSAGS_RP_FAST=0 turns both off;
DOSAGS_RP_VERIFY=1 also runs the old path and counts disagreements in a
"DOS-PORT rpcache" line):
(A) prepare_walkable_areas(), the only writer of walkable_areas_temp,
    records its inputs: a generation of thisroom.WalkAreaMask's pixels
    and the exact list of blocking rectangles it cut out. The generation
    is bumped by every path that can write those pixels (room load and
    unload, redo_walkable_areas, a drawing surface or plugin bitmap on
    the walkable mask, a restored mask). An identical input record keeps
    the temp mask's serial; any difference takes a new one. The whole
    record is compared, not hashed. The pathfinder's cache stores the
    serial it was built or verified from, and a request on the same
    serial skips the 64 KB compare.
(B) The copy-and-flood only answers "which pixels are 4-connected to the
    source". That is computed once per cache build as component labels
    over the normalised mask: every walkable pixel is 1, and Allegro's
    scanline fill spreads left/right and to overlapping runs above and
    below, never diagonally. The reachability test and the
    nearest-walkable sweep read the labels, with the same clamping,
    order and arithmetic, so they set the same suggestx/suggesty. With
    more than 65534 components it falls back to the copy and flood.
    The labels are built in two passes over each row's runs: union every
    run with the runs of the row above that share a column (the flood's
    4-connectivity). Only the runs are kept, per row (x0, x1, label); a
    pixel's label is a binary search over its row's runs, so no 128 KB
    pixel map is written or zero-filled.
    A per-pixel flood with a stack, a zero-fill and a divide per pixel
    made the first request of a mask serial ~71 ms slower on the 486
    (RP486: 244.5 vs 173.0 ms, every later request faster). The first
    request is also answered from the labels instead of building them
    and then flooding too. The build time is reported as label_us.

Gates: identical route hashes against the previous build on RT3's bench
and random requests on 5 Days', Trilby's and MMD's masks, a control that
must differ, and DOSAGS_RP_VERIFY=1 with 0 mismatches.
